## Source maps are not optional

```text
Without:  TypeError: undefined is not a function
          at t (main-a8f3.js:1:48291)      ← useless

With:     TypeError: user.permissions.map is not a function
          at UserMenu (src/features/auth/UserMenu.tsx:34:18)
```

Upload them at build time and do not serve them publicly — they expose your source. Sentry and similar tools accept an authenticated upload and keep them private.

---

## Release tagging

```js
Sentry.init({
  dsn: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN,
  release: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_COMMIT_SHA,
  environment: process.env.NODE_ENV,
  tracesSampleRate: 0.1,
  replaysOnErrorSampleRate: 1.0,
});
```

With releases tagged, a spike in a new error type maps directly to the deploy that introduced it. Without it, you are guessing.

---

## Real user monitoring

```js
import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from 'web-vitals';

[onLCP, onINP, onCLS].forEach((fn) => fn((metric) => {
  analytics.track('web-vital', {
    name: metric.name, value: metric.value, rating: metric.rating,
  });
}));
```

Lighthouse measures one run on one machine. RUM measures your actual users on actual devices and networks — and the p75 on mid-range Android is usually far worse than your laptop suggests.

---

## Noise control

A monitoring tool nobody reads is worse than none. Filter aggressively:

| Noise | Handling |
|-------|----------|
| Browser extension errors | Filter by stack frames not in your bundle |
| `ResizeObserver loop limit exceeded` | Benign — ignore it |
| Network errors from users going offline | Group and downgrade severity |
| Bot traffic | Filter by user agent |

Set alert thresholds on **rate**, not on any single occurrence. One error is noise; a 20× increase is a signal.

---

## What to actually watch

1. **Error rate per release** — did this deploy make things worse?
2. **Users affected**, not error count — one user in a loop is not an outage
3. **INP and LCP at p75** — the metrics that reflect experience
4. **Failed API calls by endpoint** — often the real cause

Session replay on errored sessions is expensive but resolves "cannot reproduce" faster than anything else.

## The failure

```text
10:00  User loads the app — index-a8f3.js, chunks hashed for that build
10:15  You deploy — new hashes, old files deleted from the CDN
10:20  User navigates to /settings → lazy import requests
       settings-4c2b.js → 404 → white screen
```

The user did nothing wrong and the error is baffling. This is one of the most common production issues in SPAs and rarely comes up until it happens.

---

## Fix 1 — keep old assets

Do not purge previous builds. Content-hashed filenames never collide, so keeping the last few deploys costs little storage and eliminates the failure entirely. Vercel and Netlify do this by default; a self-managed S3 or CDN setup usually does not, which is where teams get caught.

Retain for at least as long as a plausible session — 24 hours is a reasonable floor.

---

## Fix 2 — catch the error

```jsx
function ChunkErrorBoundary({ children }) {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary
      fallbackRender={({ error }) => {
        const isChunkError = /Loading chunk|Failed to fetch dynamically imported module/i
          .test(error.message);

        if (isChunkError) {
          return (
            <div role="alert">
              <p>A new version is available.</p>
              <button onClick={() => window.location.reload()}>Reload</button>
            </div>
          );
        }
        return <GenericError error={error} />;
      }}
    >
      {children}
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
```

An automatic reload is tempting but dangerous — if the chunk is missing for another reason you get a reload loop. Offer the button, and only auto-reload once with a flag in `sessionStorage`.

---

## Fix 3 — tell users a new version exists

```jsx
function useVersionCheck(intervalMs = 5 * 60_000) {
  const [updateAvailable, setUpdateAvailable] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const current = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_COMMIT_SHA;

    async function check() {
      if (document.hidden) return;
      try {
        const res = await fetch('/version.json', { cache: 'no-store' });
        const { version } = await res.json();
        if (version !== current) setUpdateAvailable(true);
      } catch { /* offline — ignore */ }
    }

    const id = setInterval(check, intervalMs);
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', check);
    return () => { clearInterval(id); document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', check); };
  }, [intervalMs]);

  return updateAvailable;
}
```

Show a dismissible banner rather than forcing a reload — a user mid-form will lose their work.

---

## The related problems

| Problem | Handling |
|---------|----------|
| API contract changed | Version the API; support the old shape during rollout |
| Service worker caching an old build | Version the cache name; `skipWaiting` with a user prompt |
| Long-lived WebSocket on old protocol | Version the socket messages; reconnect on mismatch |
| Cached `index.html` | `Cache-Control: no-cache` on HTML, `immutable` on hashed assets |

That last row is the configuration that makes everything else work: **never cache the HTML, always cache the hashed assets forever.**

---

## Why it matters

Teams that deploy ten times a day hit this constantly. Being able to describe it unprompted signals you have operated an application, not only built one.

## Start with data

```json
// package.json — this drives Babel, PostCSS, autoprefixer and your bundler
{
  "browserslist": [
    ">0.5%",
    "last 2 versions",
    "not dead",
    "not op_mini all"
  ]
}
```

Check what your users actually use before choosing. Supporting a browser with 0.1% share can cost 30KB of polyfills and a slower build for everyone — that is a business decision, not a technical one, so bring the numbers to it.

```bash
npx browserslist              # what does my config actually resolve to?
```

---

## Differential serving

```js
// vite.config.js
import legacy from '@vitejs/plugin-legacy';

export default {
  plugins: [legacy({ targets: ['defaults', 'not IE 11'] })],
};
```

This emits two bundles. Modern browsers get the untranspiled one via `<script type="module">`; older ones get the transpiled, polyfilled version via `nomodule`. Nobody pays for someone else's browser.

---

## Progressive enhancement

```js
// Feature-detect, never user-agent sniff
if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
  observeForLazyLoad();
} else {
  loadAllImages();          // works, just less efficiently
}

if (navigator.share) {
  await navigator.share({ url });
} else {
  await navigator.clipboard.writeText(url);
  toast('Link copied');
}
```

UA sniffing is wrong from the moment you write it — strings change, browsers lie, and a new browser you never heard of gets the fallback forever.

---

## CSS handles this natively

```css
/* Fallback first, enhancement second — old browsers ignore what they cannot parse */
.grid { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; }

@supports (display: grid) {
  .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(240px, 1fr)); }
}
```

CSS was designed to degrade. An unknown property is skipped, not fatal — which makes it the safest place to adopt new features.

---

## Polyfill only what you use

```js
// babel.config.js
{
  presets: [['@babel/preset-env', {
    useBuiltIns: 'usage',    // only polyfill what the code actually references
    corejs: 3,
  }]]
}
```

`useBuiltIns: 'entry'` includes everything for your targets; `'usage'` includes only what you use. The difference is frequently tens of kilobytes.

---

## Test it honestly

BrowserStack or Playwright against real browser versions. A VM of the oldest supported browser used once per release finds things no amount of transpilation configuration will.

---

## The conversation to have

> "Supporting this browser costs about 40KB for every user and roughly two days of engineering per quarter. It is 0.3% of sessions and 0.1% of revenue. I would propose dropping to a basic-functionality tier for it — the site works, it just is not pretty."

Framing support as tiers rather than binary is usually what unlocks the decision.
